I was stuck in your shoes for about a year playing daily. The game is 
seriously tough when you're first starting out, especially because your 
character is usually weak and you're not sure what might kill you next. 
Floating eyes would paralyze me and newts would kill me. Soldier ants and 
killer bees killed me usually at about the Oracle level. Since then, I've 
learned to play more cautiously, especially when your character is still 
weak. To me, the game is about:
1. learning what will kill you.
2. avoiding, killing or overcoming it.

I think what made a huge difference is daggers. I used to play Wizard 
constantly but never used daggers. Now instead of force bolt, I use daggers 
and become expert at them first. I save my energy for emergencies mostly or 
when I've thrown all the daggers and don't have time to re-collect them 
right away.

Descend slowly at first. Explore the entire early levels. You might find 
some useful goods, get more exp/AC, etc. plus the monsters won't be too 
tough. Stay near stairs if you're unsure or have some other escape plan 
handy.

The other thing is try not to be encumbered or worse, it slows you down. 
That led to many deaths also because I couldn't run away fast enough.

Good Luck!


